<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Auscultate — Windows installer (WiX Toolset v3).

  Built by the `windows-package` job in .github/workflows/ci.yml on top of the
  same staged folder the portable zip is made from, so the installer and the zip
  always contain the same files.

  Two preprocessor variables are required, both supplied by candle:

    ProductVersion  x.y.z, read out of the top-level CMakeLists.txt
    LicenceRtf      absolute path to the EULA converted to RTF

  The file list is NOT written here. tools/make_installer_wxs.py walks the
  staged folder and emits the ComponentGroup "StagedFiles" as a second source
  file, because what windeployqt puts in that folder changes with the Qt version
  and a hand-written list would go stale silently — as a missing Qt plugin,
  which is the kind of fault that survives every test we have and then fails on
  a user's machine.

  ===========================================================================
  Per-user, not per-machine
  ===========================================================================
  This installs to %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Auscultate and needs no elevation.
  Three reasons, in order of weight:

  1. The product ships unsigned and will stay unsigned (no budget for a
     certificate, and no certificate authority issues them free). A per-machine
     install needs elevation, and the UAC prompt for an unsigned package is the
     red-bordered "unknown publisher" one. Per-user installs raise no prompt at
     all, so the user sees SmartScreen once instead of SmartScreen plus a
     scary-looking UAC dialog. That is the only friction we can remove for
     nothing, so we remove it.
  2. Qt is LGPL-3.0 and the user has the right to replace our Qt DLLs with
     their own (ADR 0006). In %LOCALAPPDATA% they can do that with Explorer.
     Under Program Files they would need an administrator. A licence obligation
     that costs the user an admin password is one we have made harder to
     exercise than it should be.
  3. It works on a locked-down machine where the user is not an administrator,
     which is the ordinary case in a studio or a university.

  What we give up: the install is for one user, not everyone on the machine.
  For a single-user audio editor that is the normal expectation, and anyone who
  wants it elsewhere can pass INSTALLFOLDER on the msiexec command line.

  ===========================================================================
  Nothing here may defeat replacing the Qt DLLs
  ===========================================================================
  The MSI's cab is compressed, but Windows Installer expands it to ordinary
  loose files on disk, so the DLLs stay individually replaceable. Two things
  that would have broken that are deliberately avoided:

  * The Start menu shortcut is a plain shortcut, NOT an advertised one. An
    advertised shortcut is an entry point for Windows Installer resiliency:
    launching it re-checks the feature's key paths and can reinstall files.
    A user who had swapped in their own Qt6Core.dll could have had it silently
    overwritten on next launch. A plain shortcut triggers none of that.
  * Every component's key path is a registry value, never one of the shipped
    DLLs, so a replaced or deleted DLL is never what Windows Installer looks at
    when it decides whether the product is intact.

  ===========================================================================
  The installer's own third-party code
  ===========================================================================
  WiX is MS-RL. Nothing of WiX is linked into auscultate.exe — it is a build
  tool — but the .msi it produces does embed WiX's standard dialog bitmaps and
  its WixCA custom-action DLL. MS-RL reciprocity is per file and covers only
  files that contain WiX code, so it reaches those streams and not our
  executables. packaging/windows/licences/WiX-Toolset-MS-RL.txt travels with the
  product to satisfy the retain-notices condition. See third-party.json.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">

  <Product Id="*"
           Name="Auscultate"
           Language="1033"
           Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
           Manufacturer="Delta Creation Co."
           UpgradeCode="73B94798-A7DF-452C-A7DF-936586E9FF6A">

    <!-- InstallScope="perUser" sets InstallPrivileges="limited" and leaves
         ALLUSERS unset, which is what makes this a per-user install. -->
    <Package InstallerVersion="500"
             Compressed="yes"
             InstallScope="perUser"
             Manufacturer="Delta Creation Co."
             Description="Auscultate — analysis-first audio repair and mastering"
             Comments="Delta Creation Co. This package is not code-signed; see docs/INSTALLING.md." />

    <!-- Installing 0.2.0 over 0.1.0 replaces it rather than leaving two entries
         in Installed apps. AllowSameVersionUpgrades covers reinstalling the
         same version, which is what a user retrying a failed install does. -->
    <MajorUpgrade AllowSameVersionUpgrades="yes"
                  DowngradeErrorMessage="A newer version of [ProductName] is already installed. Uninstall it first if you mean to go back." />

    <MediaTemplate EmbedCab="yes" CompressionLevel="high" />

    <!-- Publisher, and no Modify button: there is one feature, so Modify has
         nothing to offer. Repair and Uninstall stay. -->
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />

    <Directory Id="TARGETDIR" Name="SourceDir">
      <Directory Id="LocalAppDataFolder">
        <!-- %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs is where per-user applications go on
             current Windows; several Microsoft-shipped apps install there. -->
        <Directory Id="ProgramsFolder" Name="Programs">
          <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Auscultate" />
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
      <!-- The shortcut goes straight into the Start menu rather than into a
           folder of its own: one application does not need a folder, and a
           folder is one more thing an uninstall can leave behind. -->
      <Directory Id="ProgramMenuFolder" />
    </Directory>

    <Component Id="ApplicationShortcut" Directory="ProgramMenuFolder" Guid="*">
      <Shortcut Id="AuscultateStartMenuShortcut"
                Name="Auscultate"
                Description="Analysis-first audio repair and mastering"
                Target="[INSTALLFOLDER]auscultate.exe"
                WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER" />
      <!-- A component that installs into the user profile must key off HKCU,
           not off a file (ICE38). It also gives the auto-GUID something stable
           to derive from. -->
      <RegistryValue Root="HKCU"
                     Key="Software\Delta Creation Co.\Auscultate Setup"
                     Name="StartMenuShortcut"
                     Type="integer"
                     Value="1"
                     KeyPath="yes" />
    </Component>

    <!-- The setup key is deliberately NOT "Software\Delta Creation Co.\Auscultate":
         that is where QSettings keeps the user's window size and preferences,
         because src/sa-ui/src/main.cpp sets the organisation to "Delta Creation
         Co." and the application to "Auscultate". Writing the installer's own
         bookkeeping there and then force-deleting the key on uninstall would
         throw away the user's settings as a side effect. A separate key keeps
         uninstall to what the installer created. -->
    <Component Id="Registration" Directory="INSTALLFOLDER" Guid="*">
      <RegistryKey Root="HKCU"
                   Key="Software\Delta Creation Co.\Auscultate Setup"
                   ForceDeleteOnUninstall="yes">
        <RegistryValue Name="InstallPath" Type="string" Value="[INSTALLFOLDER]" KeyPath="yes" />
        <RegistryValue Name="Version" Type="string" Value="$(var.ProductVersion)" />
      </RegistryKey>
      <!-- %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs is shared with other per-user applications.
           RemoveFolder removes a directory only when it is already empty, so
           this takes our folder away and leaves anyone else's alone. -->
      <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveProgramsFolder" Directory="ProgramsFolder" On="uninstall" />
    </Component>

    <Feature Id="MainApplication" Title="Auscultate" Level="1">
      <ComponentGroupRef Id="StagedFiles" />
      <ComponentRef Id="ApplicationShortcut" />
      <ComponentRef Id="Registration" />
    </Feature>

    <!-- WixUI_Minimal is welcome-plus-licence, one Install button, done. The
         licence text is docs/EULA.md converted to RTF at build time, so the
         page cannot drift from the document under review. -->
    <UIRef Id="WixUI_Minimal" />
    <WixVariable Id="WixUILicenseRtf" Value="$(var.LicenceRtf)" />

  </Product>
</Wix>
